# ungoogled-chromium / ungoogled-chromium-windows patches that Plain Browser does NOT apply.
# One entry per line, with a prefix:
#   uc:<patch>    ungoogled-chromium/patches/<patch>          ucw:<patch>  ungoogled-chromium-windows/patches/<patch>
#   prune:<file>  a Chromium file that ungoogled's pruning.list deletes and we need
#   hunks:uc:<patch>:<regex>[:!<path-regex>]  only the hunks of that upstream patch whose changed lines or file
#                 path match <regex> (scripts/patchfilter.py), except files matching <path-regex>; the rest of the
#                 patch stays applied
# prepare.py drops the patches from the upstream series and the files from pruning.list before a fresh build;
# apply-plainbrowser.ps1 reverses the patches (patch -R, last applied first) and restores the files (git checkout,
# or a download from chromium.googlesource.com) on an already-built tree; check-patches.py skips the patches' hunks.
#
# Safe Browsing: ungoogled compiles it out (safe_browsing_mode=0, hard-coded in buildflags.gni by the Windows
# patch) and deletes the code that depends on it. Plain Browser keeps Safe Browsing (local hash database through
# our proxy, patches/70-safebrowsing) and keeps ungoogled's two iridium patches that disable incident reporting
# and extended reporting.
# Windows: every hunk except signin_util_win.cc, whose hunk removes a use of a signin pref that other ungoogled
# patches delete (not Safe Browsing related).
hunks:ucw:ungoogled-chromium/windows/windows-fix-building-without-safebrowsing.patch:.:!signin_util_win\.cc
uc:core/ungoogled-chromium/fix-building-without-safebrowsing.patch
uc:core/inox-patchset/0001-fix-building-without-safebrowsing.patch
# Pruned files Safe Browsing needs back (prefs and the default download file-type policy):
prune:components/safe_browsing/core/common/safe_browsing_prefs.cc
prune:components/safe_browsing/core/common/safe_browsing_prefs.h
prune:components/safe_browsing/content/resources/empty_file_type_policy.pb
# chrome/app/etw_events/chrome_events_win.rc comes back with the Windows patch and includes these two binaries:
prune:chrome/app/etw_events/MSG00001.bin
prune:chrome/app/etw_events/chrome_events_winTEMP.BIN
# ungoogled removes the Safe Browsing preferences (registration, policy handlers, settings UI bindings, users)
# together with other unused prefs in one patch. Only its Safe Browsing hunks are dropped; the rest stays.
# The regex also catches the reporting and password-protection prefs that live outside the safe_browsing dirs
# (security_interstitials controller clients, policy handler list, settings UI).
hunks:uc:core/ungoogled-chromium/remove-unused-preferences-fields.patch:safe_browsing|SafeBrowsing|safebrowsing|ExtendedReporting|PasswordProtection|EnhancedProtection
# Moves kJavascriptOptimizerBlockedForUnfamiliarSites out of the (pruned) Safe Browsing prefs; with those prefs
# back the upstream code is right and the move would define the constant twice.
uc:core/ungoogled-chromium/move-js-optimizer-unfamiliar-sites.patch
# Secure DNS: ungoogled's doh-changes.patch turns DNS-over-HTTPS off and disables the automatic-mode upgrade table
# (kDnsOverHttpsUpgrade), because that table listed Google Public DNS. The same patch also deletes the Google
# entries from the table (net/dns/public/doh_provider_entry.cc) and the google.com probe host; those hunks stay.
# With these two hunks dropped, secure DNS is back in Chromium's default "automatic" mode: a lookup goes over
# DoH only when the resolver the system already uses has a DoH endpoint in the table (Cloudflare, Quad9, ...),
# otherwise as before. No new party sees the queries. Off on domain-joined machines and with Windows parental
# controls (upstream behaviour). docs/PRIVACY.md, "Secure DNS".
hunks:uc:core/ungoogled-chromium/doh-changes.patch:default_dns_over_https_config_source\.cc|services/network/public/cpp/features\.cc
